## Root-Cause / Architectural Analysis

The button system is already correctly centralized: `src/components/ui/button.jsx` is the single CVA-based source of truth, consumed by 10+ known callers (MFASetup, alert-dialog, calendar, carousel, pagination, sidebar, AuditLog, ChangePassword, plus the composite surfaces you named — Monthly Calendar, Room Board, OTA Channels). This is the correct pressure point: upgrading the CVA definition propagates to every consumer with zero page edits. No caller reads individual class tokens, so the only hard invariant is the existing test suite in `button.test.jsx`.

Key structural constraints I traced:

1. Test-lock on class tokens. `button.test.jsx` asserts exact class presence per variant: `bg-destructive`, `border`, `bg-secondary`, `hover:bg-accent`, `text-primary`, `h-9/w-9`, `rounded-md`. The refactor must be additive over these tokens — I keep every asserted class and layer luxury styling (gradient, bevel, shadow, transform) on top. Removing any of them is the failure mode that would break the suite.

2. Theme-token boundary. Callers depend on `--primary/--destructive/--secondary` semantic tokens. I preserve `bg-primary`, `bg-destructive`, `bg-secondary` as fallbacks and paint gradients over them, so theme swaps degrade gracefully instead of hard-coding away the token contract.

3. Motion boundary with framer-motion. Several callers wrap buttons in `motion.*`. Applying blanket `button { transition/transform }` in `index.css` would fight framer's transform track. The transform/elevation therefore lives on the component classes (self-contained, `transform-gpu`), and global CSS only adds a reduced-motion guard plus optional utilities — no global `button {}` transform rule.

4. Focus-ring invariant. Base swaps the generic `ring-ring` for the brand emerald `--brand #00E096` ring; this is a purely visual change with no caller dependency.

Below are the surgical patches.

