## Root-Cause & Architecture Analysis

This is not a defect fix — it is a centralized design-system upgrade. My analysis focuses on where risk actually lives, since the "surgical" mandate depends on getting the isolation boundaries right.

Behavioral contract (the real constraint)

`src/components/ui/button.jsx` is a pure, stateless presentational primitive. There is no runtime state to isolate and no async path that warrants an error boundary. The isolation surface that matters is the implicit class-token API. Tracing the callers you listed (MFASetup, alert-dialog, calendar, carousel, pagination, sidebar, AuditLog, ChangePassword) plus `button.test.jsx`, two things are load-bearing:

1. The CVA class output — `button.test.jsx` asserts on specific tokens: `inline-flex`, `items-center`, `bg-destructive`, `border`, `bg-secondary`, `hover:bg-accent`, `text-primary`, `rounded-md`, `h-9`, `w-9`. If I swap these for gradients/arbitrary values, the tests break and the task's "tests continue to work flawlessly" requirement fails.
2. `asChild` / `Slot` merging — `alert-dialog`, `sidebar`, and `pagination` render Button as a Slot onto anchors/links. Any transform (`translate-y`) I add must be neutralized by `disabled` AND must not fight framer-motion transforms on parent wrappers.

Root architectural decision

Rather than replace the base color tokens, I layer the 3D treatment on top of them. `bg-destructive`/`bg-secondary` are `background-color`; the gradients are `background-image` — they occupy different tailwind-merge groups, so `cn()` keeps both. This preserves every existing test token while the gradient renders over the solid color as a safe fallback. This is the surgical path: zero caller changes, zero test regressions, one file drives Monthly Calendar / Room Board / OTA Channels centrally.

Two failure modes I explicitly guard against

- Transform leakage on disabled/reduced-motion: `disabled:translate-y-0` and `motion-reduce:*` overrides prevent the tactile transform from firing where it must not.
- framer-motion conflict: the elevation uses `translate-y` (a transform). If a page wraps a Button in `motion.div` and animates transform, they compose on separate elements, so no conflict — but a Button used *as* a motion child would collide. The index.css rule scopes elevation transitions to the button element only and never sets transform globally.

I do not have `src/index.css` contents in evidence, so its patch is additive-only (appended, namespaced) to avoid clobbering the `--s-canvas`/`--brand` tokens.

